{"title":"KVPY","category":"Engineering","bank":"kvpy","subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","count":27,"questions":[{"id":51112,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"A constant current $(0.5 \\mathrm{amp})$ is passed for 1 hour through (i) aqueous $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$, (ii) aqueous $\\mathrm{CuSO}_{4}$ and (iii) molten $\\mathrm{AlF}_{3}$, separately. The ratio of the mass of the metals deposited on the cathode is\n$\\left[\\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Ag}}, \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Cu},}, \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Al}}\\right.$ are molar masses of the respective metals]","options":["$\\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Ag}}: 2 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Cu}}: 3 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Al}}$","$\\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Ag}}: \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Cu}}: \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{A} 1}$","$6 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Ag}}: 3 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Cu}}: 2 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{A} 1}$","$3 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Ag}}: 2 \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Cu}}: \\mathrm{M}_{\\mathrm{Al}}$"],"answer":2,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F5_MElYA-qIvsN0JFXhRtJkY4oTFBGpLou51d1miYZYY.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F5_MElYA-qIvsN0JFXhRtJkY4oTFBGpLou51d1miYZYY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F5_MElYA-qIvsN0JFXhRtJkY4oTFBGpLou51d1miYZYY.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (5 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2137d9b4281bed046e9"},{"id":51113,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Upon dipping a copper rod, the aqueous solution of the salt that can turn blue is:-","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Ca</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Mg</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Zn</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>AgNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></math>"],"answer":3,"solution":"<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi>AgNO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mi>Cu</mi><mo>⟶</mo><mi>Ag</mi><mo>+</mo><mi>Cu</mi><msub><mfenced><msub><mi>NO</mi><mn>3</mn></msub></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msub></math><br />\nMetal can reduce that metal cation which is placed below it in reactivity series.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2020 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da748dbae4746281101b3"},{"id":51114,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"For electroplating, $1.5$ amp current is passed for 250 s through $250 \\mathrm{mI}$. of $0.15 \\mathrm{M}$ solution of $\\mathrm{MSO}_{4}$. Only $85 \\%$ of the current was utilized for electrolysis. The molarity of $\\mathrm{MSO}_{4}$ solution after electrolysis is closest to [Assume that the volume of the solution remained constant]","options":["$0.14$","$0.014$","$0.07$","$0.035$"],"answer":0,"solution":"No. of mole $=\\frac{\\mathrm{i} \\times \\mathrm{t}}{96500 \\times \\mathrm{v} . \\mathrm{f}}, \\quad \\mathrm{n} \\times \\mathrm{v} . \\mathrm{f}=\\frac{\\mathrm{i} \\times \\mathrm{t}}{96500} \\times \\mathrm{n}=\\frac{1.5 \\times 250}{96500 \\times 2} \\times 0.85\\mathrm{n}=.00165$ (deposited)\n$\\mathrm{n}_{\\mathrm{i}}=\\frac{250 \\times .15}{1000}=.0375$ (initial mole)\n$\\mathrm{n}_{\\text {left }}=.03585$ (mole left)\n$\\mathrm{M}_{\\text {left }}=\\frac{.03585}{.25}=0.143$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2130f3e769da28dc30e"},{"id":51115,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The limiting molar conductivities of the given electrolytes at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ follow the order\n\n\\begin{array}{l}\n{\\left[\\lambda^{0}\\left(\\mathrm{~K}^{+}\\right)=73.5, \\lambda^{0}\\left(\\mathrm{Cl}^{-}\\right)=76.3\\right.} \\\\\n\\left.\\lambda^{0}\\left(\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+}\\right)=119.0, \\lambda^{0}\\left(\\mathrm{SO}_{4}^{2-}\\right)=160.0 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right]\n\\end{array}\n","options":["$\\mathrm{KCl} \\lt  \\mathrm{CaCl}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}$","$\\mathrm{KCl} \\lt  \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\lt  \\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}$","$\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\lt  \\mathrm{CaCl}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{KCl}$","$\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2} \\lt  \\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4} \\lt  \\mathrm{KCl}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{KCl}}=73.5+76.3=149.8\\lambda_{\\mathrm{CaCl}_{2}}^{\\circ}=119+2 \\times 76.3=271.6\\lambda^{\\circ}{ }_{\\mathrm{K}_{2} \\mathrm{SO}_{4}}=2 \\times 73.5+160=307$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b3fb51eebb7e5b56"},{"id":51116,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The reducing ability of the metals $\\mathrm{K}, \\mathrm{Au}, \\mathrm{Zn}$ and $\\mathrm{Pb}$ follons the order","options":["$K\\gt P b\\gt A u\\gt Z n$","$\\mathrm{Pb}\\gt \\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Zn}\\gt \\mathrm{Au}$","$\\mathrm{Zn}\\gt \\mathrm{Au}\\gt \\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Pb}$","$\\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Zn}\\gt \\mathrm{Pb}\\gt \\mathrm{Au}$"],"answer":3,"solution":"Theoritical","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213e17e7bfb5ea07146","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51117,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The following reaction takes place at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ in an electrochemical cell involving two metals $\\mathrm{A}$ and B,\n$\\mathrm{A}^{2+}(\\mathrm{aq} .)+\\mathrm{B}(\\mathrm{s}) \\rightarrow \\mathrm{B}^{2+}($ aq $)+\\mathrm{A}(\\mathrm{s})$\nWith $\\left[\\mathrm{A}^{2+}\\right]=4 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$ and $\\left[\\mathrm{B}^{2+}\\right]=2 \\times 10^{-3} \\mathrm{M}$ in the respective half-cells, the cell EMF is $1.091 \\mathrm{~V}$. The equilibrium constant of the reaction is closest to","options":["$4 \\times 10^{36}$","$2 \\times 10^{37}$","$2 \\times 10^{34}$","$4 \\times 10^{37}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}=\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\circ}-\\frac{.0591}{2} \\log \\frac{2 \\times 10^{-3}}{4 \\times 10^{-3}}1.091=\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}{ }_{\\text {cell }}-\\frac{.0591}{2} \\log (.5)\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\circ}=1.099\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\circ}=-\\frac{.0591}{2} \\log \\mathrm{k}\\log \\mathrm{k}=-\\frac{1.099 \\times 2}{.0591}=-37.22\\therefore \\mathrm{k}=2 \\times 10^{37}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213da3f115cbcbfb4a3","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51118,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Which of the following transformations can be carried out by using HI as a rducing agent, under acidic conditions?\n[Given : $\\left.\\left.I_{2}(s) \\rightarrow 2\\right|^{-} E^{\\theta}=0.54 \\mathrm{~V}\\right]$\n(i) $\\mathrm{Cu}^{+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}(\\mathrm{s}) \\quad \\mathrm{E}^{\\theta}=0.52 \\mathrm{~V}$\n(ii) $\\mathrm{Cr}^{3+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Cr}^{2+} \\mathrm{E}^{\\theta}=-0.41 \\mathrm{~V}$\n(iii) $\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} \\mathrm{E}^{\\theta}=0.77 \\mathrm{~V}$\n(iv) $\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{s}) \\mathrm{E}^{\\theta}=-0.44 \\mathrm{~V}$","options":["(i) and (iii)","(ii) and (iv)","only (iii)","only (ii)"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}$ is having maximum SRP value.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2017 (19 Nov SA)","_marksId":"6174d213051e5a5a47663adf"},{"id":51119,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Electrolysis of concentrated aqueous solution of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>NaCl</mi></math> results in","options":["increase in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> of the solution","decrease in <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> of the solution","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> liberation at the cathode","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math> liberation at the anode"],"answer":0,"solution":"<p>The cathode half-reaction in the process is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math></p>\n\n<p>which increases <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></mfenced></math> and, therefore <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> and the anode half-reaction.</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><msup><mi>Cl</mi><mo>-</mo></msup><mo>⟶</mo><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">e</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math> does not produce or<br />\nconsume <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> or <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>OH</mi><mo>-</mo></msup></math>.</p>\n\n<p>Overall, the reaction can be written as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mi>NaCl</mi><mo>+</mo><mn>2</mn><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi><mo>⟶</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>NaOH</mi><mo>+</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mo>+</mo><msub><mi>Cl</mi><mn>2</mn></msub></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2019 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da6f7a58ccd5b49c52c80"},{"id":51120,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The specific conductance ( $\\kappa$ ) of $0.02 \\mathrm{M}$ aqueous acetic acid solution at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $1.65 \\times 10^{-4} \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{-1}$. The degree of dissociation of acetic acid is\n[Given : equivalent conductance at infinite dilution of $\\mathrm{H}^{+}=349.1 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}=40.9 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2}\\left.\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\\right]$","options":["$0.021$","$0.21$","$0.012$","$0.12$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\lambda_{\\mathrm{M}}=\\frac{1000 \\times \\mathrm{K}}{\\mathrm{M}}=\\frac{1000 \\times 1.65 \\times 10^{-4}}{0.2}=8.25\\lambda_{\\mathrm{H}}^{\\infty}=\\lambda_{\\mathrm{M}}^{\\infty}\\left(\\mathrm{H}^{+}\\right)+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{M}}^{\\infty}\\left(\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}\\right)=349.1+40.9=390\\propto=\\frac{8.25}{390}=.0211$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213ac7606c4c03bde2c"},{"id":51121,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The standard reduction potentials (in V) of a few metal ion/metal electrodes are given below.\nThe reducing strength of the metals follows the order.","options":["$\\mathrm{Ag}\\gt \\mathrm{Cu}\\gt \\mathrm{Pb}\\gt \\mathrm{Cr}$","$\\mathrm{Cr}\\gt \\mathrm{Pb}\\gt \\mathrm{Cu}\\gt \\mathrm{Ag}$","$\\mathrm{Pb}\\gt \\mathrm{Cr}\\gt \\mathrm{Ag}\\gt \\mathrm{Cu}$","$\\mathrm{Cr}\\gt \\mathrm{Ag}\\gt \\mathrm{Cu}\\gt \\mathrm{Pb}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"Which have low value of reduction potential is a good reducing agent.\n\n\\mathrm{Cr}>\\mathrm{Pb}>\\mathrm{Cu}>\\mathrm{Ag}\n","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213d26613b93cc80ffe"},{"id":51122,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The molar conductivities of $\\mathrm{HCl}, \\mathrm{NaCl}, \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$, and $\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$ at infinite dilution follow the order","options":["$\\mathrm{HCl}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\gt \\mathrm{NaCl}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$","$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}\\gt \\mathrm{HCl}\\gt \\mathrm{NaCl}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}$","$\\mathrm{HCl}\\gt \\mathrm{NaCl}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}$","$\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COOH}\\gt \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COONa}\\gt \\mathrm{HCl}\\gt \\mathrm{NaCl}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\lambda_{\\infty} \\quad \\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{COO}^{-}=40.9 \\times 10^{-4} \\quad \\mathrm{Sm}^{2} \\quad \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\lambda_{\\infty} \\quad \\mathrm{Na}^{+}=50.10 \\times 10^{-4} \\quad \\mathrm{Sm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\lambda_{\\infty} \\quad \\mathrm{Cl}^{-} \\quad=\\quad 76.35 \\times 10^{-4} \\quad \\mathrm{Sm}^{2} \\quad \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\lambda_{\\infty} \\quad \\mathrm{H}^{+}=349 \\times 10^{-4} \\quad \\mathrm{Sm}^{2} \\quad \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\lambda_{x} \\mathrm{HCl}=\\lambda_{u^{t}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{c}^{-}} / \\lambda_{\\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{coon}}=\\lambda_{\\mathrm{al}, \\mathrm{coo}^{-}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{u}^{+}}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{Ncl}}=\\lambda_{\\mathrm{N}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{c} \\mathrm{r}^{-}} / \\lambda_{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{com} \\mathrm{N}}=\\lambda_{\\mathrm{CH}_{3} \\mathrm{coo}^{-}}+\\lambda_{\\mathrm{N}^{+}}\\lambda_{\\text {HC }}=425.35 \\mathrm{sm}^{2} / \\mathrm{mol}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{cH}_{3} \\mathrm{coon}}=389.9 \\mathrm{sm}^{2} / \\mathrm{mol}\\lambda_{\\text {Nacl }}=126.45 \\mathrm{sm}^{2} / \\mathrm{mol}\\lambda_{\\mathrm{Cl}, \\mathrm{coON}}=91 \\mathrm{sm}^{2} / \\mathrm{mol}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213098606ea917d26e6"},{"id":51123,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The standard cell potential for $Z n\\left|Z n^{2+} \\| C u^{2+}\\right| C u$ is $1.10 \\mathrm{~V}$. When the cell is completely discharged, $\\log \\left[\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\right] /\\left[\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\\right]$ is closest to","options":["$37.3$","$0.026$","$18.7$","$0.052$"],"answer":0,"solution":"<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252FcpDoECTOHzkJmj3n8vCowJnCEXW4LP_Bm796Ub76718.original.fullsize.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FcpDoECTOHzkJmj3n8vCowJnCEXW4LP_Bm796Ub76718.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2FcpDoECTOHzkJmj3n8vCowJnCEXW4LP_Bm796Ub76718.original.fullsize.png?alt=media\">","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213f2438130e4e52e1d"},{"id":51124,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The amount of Ag (atomic mass $=108$ ) deposited at the cathode when a current of $0.5 \\mathrm{amp}$ is passed through a solution of $\\mathrm{AgNO}_{3}$ for 1 hour is closest to:","options":["$2 \\mathrm{~g}$","$5 \\mathrm{~g}$","$108 \\mathrm{~g}$","$11 \\mathrm{~g}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\mathrm{W}=\\frac{\\mathrm{E}}{96500} \\times \\mathrm{I} \\times \\mathrm{t}\\mathrm{W}=\\frac{108}{96500} \\times 0.5 \\times 3600=2 \\mathrm{gm}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2135bea192d29866785"},{"id":51125,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among $\\mathrm{Mg}, \\mathrm{Cu}, \\mathrm{Fe}, \\mathrm{Zn}$, the metal that does not produce hydrogen gas in reaction with hydrochloric acid is.","options":["$\\mathrm{Cu}$","$\\mathrm{Zn}$","$\\mathrm{Mg}$","$\\mathrm{Fe}$"],"answer":0,"solution":"Metals having more standard reduction potential than $\\mathrm{H}^{+} / \\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$ can't produce $\\mathrm{H}_{2}(\\mathrm{~g})$ in acidic medium.","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2015 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d213dfda6a311f931d94"},{"id":51126,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The standard electrode potential of $\\mathrm{Zn}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Zn}$ is $-0.76 \\mathrm{~V}$ and that of $\\mathrm{Ca}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}$ is $0.34 \\mathrm{~V}$. The emf $(\\mathrm{V})$ and the free energy change $\\left(\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}\\right)$, respectively, for a Daniel cell will be","options":["$-0.42$ and 81","$1.1$ and -213","$-1.1$ and 213","$0.42$ and $-81$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Cell}}^{0}=\\mathrm{E}^{0} \\mathrm{Cu}^{2+}\\left|\\mathrm{Cu}+\\mathrm{E}^{0} \\mathrm{Zn}\\right| \\mathrm{Zn}^{2+}\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{cell}}^{0}=0.34+0.76=+1.1 \\mathrm{~V}\\Delta G=-2 \\times 96500 \\times 1.1=-213 V$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213c3eb0b41c31d47ea"},{"id":51127,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The standard free energy change (in $J)$ for the reaction $3 \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}(\\mathrm{aq})+2 \\mathrm{Cr}(\\mathrm{s})=2 \\mathrm{Cr}^{3+}(\\mathrm{aq})+3 \\mathrm{Fe}(\\mathrm{s})$ given","options":["57,900","$-57,900$","$-173,700$","173,700"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Cell}}^{\\circ}=\\mathrm{SRP}$ cathode - SRP anode $=-0.44-(-0.74)=0.3 \\mathrm{~V}\\underset{Y C}{\\Delta} \\mathrm{G}=-\\mathrm{nF} \\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Cel|}}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{n}=6\\right.$ ele $\\left.^{-}\\right)$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2131851ddf34ec979e4"},{"id":51128,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The density of eq. wt of a metal are $10.5 \\mathrm{gcm}^{-3}$ and 100 , respectively. The time required for a current of 3 amp to deposit a $0.005 \\mathrm{~mm}$ thick layer of the same metal on an area of $80 \\mathrm{~cm}^{2}$ is closest to","options":["$120 \\mathrm{~s}$","$135 \\mathrm{~s}$","$67.5 \\mathrm{~s}$","$270 \\mathrm{~s}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{w}=\\frac{\\mathrm{E} \\times \\mathrm{i} \\times \\mathrm{t}}{96500}\\mathrm{w}=\\frac{100 \\times 3 \\times \\mathrm{t}}{96500}\\mathrm{~d}=\\frac{\\mathrm{m}}{\\mathrm{V}}\\mathrm{w}=10.5 \\times 80 \\times 5 \\times 10^{-4}=42 \\times 10^{-2}42 \\times 10^{-2}=\\frac{100 \\times 3 \\times \\mathrm{t}}{96500}\\mathrm{t}=135 \\mathrm{sec}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d21309083ac41c2ad06c"},{"id":51129,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The order of reactivity of $\\mathrm{K}, \\mathrm{Mg}, \\mathrm{Au}$ and $\\mathrm{Zn}$ with water is","options":["$\\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Zn}\\gt \\mathrm{Mg}\\gt \\mathrm{Au}$","$\\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Mg}\\gt \\mathrm{Zn}\\gt \\mathrm{Au}$","$\\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Au}\\gt \\mathrm{Mg}\\gt \\mathrm{Zn}$","$\\mathrm{Au}\\gt \\mathrm{Zn}\\gt \\mathrm{K}\\gt \\mathrm{Mg}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {red }}^{0}=\\mathrm{K} \\lt  \\mathrm{Mg} \\lt  \\mathrm{Zn} \\lt  \\mathrm{Au}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d2136e6782a6cfd83f82"},{"id":51130,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The value of the limiting molar conductivity $\\left(\\Lambda^{\\circ}\\right)$ for $\\mathrm{NaCl}, \\mathrm{HCl}$ and $\\mathrm{NaOAc}$ are 126.4, $425.9$ and $91.0 \\mathrm{~S} \\mathrm{~cm}^{2}\\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$, respectively. For HOAc, $\\Lambda^{\\circ}$ in $\\mathrm{S} \\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}$ is","options":["$390.5$","$299.5$","$208.5$","$217.4$"],"answer":0,"solution":"$\\begin{aligned} \\Lambda(\\mathrm{HOAc}) &=\\Lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{NaOAc})+\\Lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{HCl})-\\Lambda^{\\circ}(\\mathrm{NaCl}) \\\\ &=91+425.9-126.4=390.5 \\end{aligned}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2131c292c2f96560081"},{"id":51131,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The standard Gibbs free energy change $\\left(\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}\\right.$ in $\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ), in a Daniel cell $\\left(\\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\circ}=1.1 \\mathrm{~V}\\right.$ ), when 2 moles of $\\mathrm{Zn}(\\mathrm{s})$ is oxidized at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$, is closest to","options":["$-212.3$","$-106.2$","$-424.6$","$-53.1$"],"answer":2,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Zn}+\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Zn}^{+2}+\\mathrm{Cu}2 \\mathrm{Zn}+2 \\mathrm{Cu}^{+2} \\rightarrow 2 \\mathrm{Zn}^{+2}+2 \\mathrm{Cu}$\nFor 2 moles of $\\mathrm{Zn}, \\mathrm{n}=4\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{nFE}_{\\mathrm{Cell}}^{\\circ}=-4 \\times 96500 \\times 1.1=-424.6 \\mathrm{~kJ}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2137cadd9bc04ac1ec8"},{"id":51132,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"If $E_{\\mathrm{Fe}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Fe}}^{\\circ}=-0.440 \\mathrm{~V}$ and $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Fe}^{2+}}^{\\circ}=0.770 \\mathrm{~V}$, then $\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{Fe}^{3+} / \\mathrm{Fe}}^{o}$ is-","options":["$0.330 \\mathrm{~V}$","$-0.037 \\mathrm{~V}$","$-0.330 \\mathrm{~V}$","$-1.210 \\mathrm{~V}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3} / \\mathrm{Fe}\\mathrm{Fe}^{+2}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe} \\quad \\mathrm{E}^{0}=-0.44\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}^{+2} \\quad \\mathrm{E}^{0}=0.770\\overline{\\mathrm{Fe}^{+3}+3 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\rightarrow \\mathrm{Fe}} \\quad \\mathrm{E}^{0}=?\\overline{\\mathrm{E}^{0}=\\frac{\\mathrm{n}_{1} \\mathrm{E}_{1}^{0}+\\mathrm{n}_{2} \\mathrm{E}_{2}^{0}}{\\mathrm{n}_{3}}}=\\frac{2(-0.44)+1 \\times(0.770)}{3}=\\frac{-0.88+0.770}{3}=-0.037 \\mathrm{volt}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2011 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213b4f582cbc4c20ee5"},{"id":51133,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Among the following, the plot that correctly represents the conductometric titration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> is","options":["<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fb2a071af-5372-4348-95d6-72d73744bb9c-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb2a071af-5372-4348-95d6-72d73744bb9c-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fb2a071af-5372-4348-95d6-72d73744bb9c-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Ff9f0c2b8-5235-489c-a83b-2766d4147706-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Ff9f0c2b8-5235-489c-a83b-2766d4147706-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Ff9f0c2b8-5235-489c-a83b-2766d4147706-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252F5fe63923-b76d-45aa-b0cb-9508bfe6a996-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F5fe63923-b76d-45aa-b0cb-9508bfe6a996-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2F5fe63923-b76d-45aa-b0cb-9508bfe6a996-image.png?alt=media\"/>","<img src=\"/api/proxy-image?url=https%3A%2F%2Ffirebasestorage.googleapis.com%2Fv0%2Fb%2Fquantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app%2Fo%2Fquestions%252Ffigs%252Fpyq%252Fkvpy%252Fa677a861-a9d2-4b94-b818-1424c262f30a-image.png%3Falt%3Dmedia&clean=1&v=wm2\" data-qx-orig-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa677a861-a9d2-4b94-b818-1424c262f30a-image.png?alt=media\" data-qx-storage-src=\"https://firebasestorage.googleapis.com/v0/b/quantrexacademy-app.firebasestorage.app/o/questions%2Ffigs%2Fpyq%2Fkvpy%2Fa677a861-a9d2-4b94-b818-1424c262f30a-image.png?alt=media\"/>"],"answer":1,"solution":"<p>The principle of conductometric titration is based on the fact that during the titration one of the ions is replaced by other and invariably these two ions differ in the ionic conductivity.</p>\n\n<p>In the conductometric titration of <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>05</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></math> <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><msub><mi>SO</mi><mn>4</mn></msub></math> with <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi><mo> </mo><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math>, initially the fast moving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></math> ion get neutralised as <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">O</mi></math> and is replaced by slow moving <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msubsup><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn><mo>+</mo></msubsup></math> ion up to neutralisation point. After neutralisation point, weak electrolyte <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi>NH</mi><mn>4</mn></msub><mi>OH</mi></math> is added gradually, which do not affect the conductance.</p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da68b133e4f664c903217"},{"id":51134,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"The equilibrium constant of a 2 electron redox reaction at $298 \\mathrm{~K}$ is $3.8 \\times 10^{-3}$. The cell potential $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}$ (in $\\left.\\mathrm{V}\\right)$ and the free energy change $\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ}$ (in $\\mathrm{kJ} \\mathrm{mol}^{-1}$ ) for this equilibrium respectively, are","options":["$-0.071,-13.8$","$-0.071,13.8$","$0.71,-13.8$","$0.071,-13.8$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\begin{array}{rl}\\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ} & =-2.303 \\times 8.314 \\times 298 \\log \\left(3.8 \\times 10^{-3}\\right) \\mathrm{J} \\\\ & =13809.3876 \\mathrm{~J}=13.809 \\mathrm{Kg} \\\\ \\Delta \\mathrm{G}^{\\circ} & =-\\mathrm{nFE}^{\\circ} \\\\ 1380 & 9.387=-2 \\times 96500 \\times \\mathrm{E}^{\\circ} \\\\ \\mathrm{E}_{\\text {cell }}^{\\circ} & =.071\\end{array}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2139269411913facad3"},{"id":51135,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Given the standard potentials $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}\\right)$ and $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{+} / \\mathrm{Cu}\\right)$ as $0.340 \\mathrm{~V}$ and $0.522 \\mathrm{~V}$ respectively, the value of $\\mathrm{E}^{\\circ}\\left(\\mathrm{Cu}^{2+} / \\mathrm{Cu}^{+}\\right)$is","options":["$0.364 \\mathrm{~V}$","$0.158 \\mathrm{~V}$","$-0.182 \\mathrm{~V}$","$-0.316 \\mathrm{~V}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2}+2 \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu} \\Delta \\mathrm{G}_{1}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{n}_{1} \\mathrm{E}_{1}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{F}\\frac{\\mathrm{Cu}+2} + \\mathrm{e}^{-} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu} \\Delta \\mathrm{G}_{2}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{n}_{2} \\mathrm{E}_{2}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{F}\\mathrm{Cu}^{+2}+\\mathrm{e}^{-} \\longrightarrow \\mathrm{Cu}^{+} \\Delta \\mathrm{G}_{3}^{\\circ}=-\\mathrm{n}_{3} \\mathrm{E}_{3}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{F}\\Delta \\mathrm{G}_{3}^{\\circ}=\\Delta \\mathrm{G}_{1}^{\\circ}-\\Delta \\mathrm{G}_{2}^{\\circ}-\\mathrm{n}_{3} \\mathrm{E}_{3}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{F}=-\\mathrm{n}_{1} \\mathrm{E}_{1}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{F}+\\mathrm{n}_{2} \\mathrm{E}_{2}^{\\circ} \\mathrm{F}\\mathrm{E}_{3}^{\\circ}=\\frac{\\mathrm{n}_{1} \\mathrm{E}_{1}^{\\circ}-\\mathrm{n}_{2} \\mathrm{E}_{2}^{\\circ}}{\\mathrm{n}_{3}}=\\frac{2 \\times 0.34-.522}{1}=.158 \\mathrm{~V}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2016 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d213e13e061ba9f109c4"},{"id":51136,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"<p>For the electrochemical cell shown below</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Pt</mi><mo>|</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>atm</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>|</mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>.</mo><mo>,</mo><mi>xM</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>|</mo></math><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"|\" open=\"|\"><mrow><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>,</mo><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow></mfenced></mrow></mfenced><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p>The potential is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>49</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math> at <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>298</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi></math>. The <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>pH</mi></math> of the solution is closest to [ Given, standard reduction potential, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>°</mo></math> for <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mo>/</mo><mi>Cu</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>34</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">V</mi></math>Gas constant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>31</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">J</mi><mo> </mo><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">K</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> Faraday constant, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">F</mi></math> is <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>9</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>65</mn><mo>×</mo><msup><mn>10</mn><mn>4</mn></msup><msup><mi>JV</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup><mo> </mo><msup><mi>mol</mi><mrow><mo>-</mo><mn>1</mn></mrow></msup></math> ]</p>","options":["<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>1</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>8</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>3</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>5</mn></math>","<math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>3</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>2</mn></math>"],"answer":2,"solution":"<p>According to Nernst equation,</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>cell</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>°</mo></msup><mi>cell</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0591</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mfrac><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">P</mi></mfenced><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">R</mi></mfenced></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p>For the given electrochemical cell</p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mi>Pt</mi><mo>∣</mo><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mn>2</mn></msub><mfenced><mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">p</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>1</mn><mo> </mo><mi>atm</mi></mrow></mfenced><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>·</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>‖</mo><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup><mfenced><mrow><mi>aq</mi><mo>·</mo><mn>10</mn><mo> </mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">M</mi></mrow></mfenced><mo>/</mo><mi>Cu</mi><mfenced><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">s</mi></mfenced></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><msub><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mi>cell</mi></msub><mo>=</mo><msub><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">E</mi><mo>°</mo></msup><mi>cell</mi></msub><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0591</mn></mrow><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">n</mi></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mfrac><msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mn>2</mn></msup><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi>Cu</mi><mrow><mn>2</mn><mo>+</mo></mrow></msup></mfenced></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>34</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0591</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mi>log</mi><mfrac><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi><mn>2</mn></msup><mn>1</mn></mfrac></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>49</mn><mo>=</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>34</mn><mo>-</mo><mfrac><mrow><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0591</mn></mrow><mn>2</mn></mfrac><mo>×</mo><mn>2</mn><mi>logx</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>15</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mn>0</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>0591</mn><mo>×</mo><mi>logx</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>53</mn><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>logx</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Also <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>,</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>log</mi><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi>pH</mi></math></p>\n\n<p>Here, <math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mfenced close=\"]\" open=\"[\"><msup><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">H</mi><mo>+</mo></msup></mfenced><mo>=</mo><mi mathvariant=\"normal\">x</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>∴</mo><mo> </mo><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mo>-</mo><mi>logx</mi></math></p>\n\n<p><math xmlns=\"http://www.w3.org/1998/Math/MathML\"><mo>⇒</mo><mi>pH</mi><mo>=</mo><mn>2</mn><mo>.</mo><mn>53</mn></math></p>","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2018 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"614da692f39ceb57b71c95d7","_resolvedFrom":"marks_login","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51137,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"If the molar conductivities (in $\\left.\\mathrm{S} \\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$ of $\\mathrm{NaCl}, \\mathrm{KCl}$ and $\\mathrm{NaOH}$ at infinite dilution are 126,150 and 250 respectively, the molar conductivity of $\\mathrm{KOH}\\left(\\right.$ in $\\left.\\mathrm{S} \\mathrm{cm}^{2} \\mathrm{~mol}^{-1}\\right)$ :","options":["526","226","26","274"],"answer":3,"solution":"No Solution","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2014 (SB/SX)","_marksId":"6174d2136e89dcbd7b7b2ac3","questionType":"singleCorrect","type":"singleCorrect"},{"id":51138,"subject":"Chemistry","chapter":"Electrochemistry","exam":"Engineering","examName":"KVPY","q":"Which of the following metals will precipitate copper from copper sulphate solution?","options":["$\\mathrm{Hg}$","$\\mathrm{Sn}$","$\\mathrm{Au}$","$\\mathrm{Pt}$"],"answer":1,"solution":"$\\mathrm{E}_{\\mathrm{red}}^{0}=\\mathrm{Sn} \\lt  \\mathrm{Cu} \\lt  \\mathrm{Hg} \\lt  \\mathrm{Au}$","difficulty":"Medium","source":"KVPY 2013 (SA)","_marksId":"6174d21386ba95079398fbd7"}]}